<!-- Chapter 5 exercises.

     One flat list, numbered in order. Which exercises get assigned
     together lives in the course schedule, not here.

     Exercises 1-5 go with Sections 5.1-5.4 of the book (pages 1-3 of
     this chapter: page1-mixed-strategies, page2-tennis-other-methods,
     page3-poker). Exercises for 5.5 onward can be appended below;
     numbering of the existing ones will not shift.

     Sources:
       1. Computing a Mixed Equilibrium   - previous HW 8, Exercise 1
                                            (old part (c) is now a remark)
       2. A Symmetric 3x3 Game            - previous HW 8, Exercise 2
                                            (new numbers; set up, then solve)
       3. Active and Inactive Strategies  - previous HW 8, Problem 1
       4. A Two-Stage Prisoner's Dilemma  - previous HW 8, Problem 2
                                            (strategies renamed C, D, T;
                                            moves are lowercase c, d)
       5. A Simple Poker Game, Revisited  - new; Section 5.3.1 method,
                                            finishes hw-simple-poker (Ch. 4)

     Exercises 6-9 go with Sections 5.5-5.6 (pages 4-5:
     page4-zero-sum, page5-ultimatum-water-pollution).
       6. Maximins and Theorem 5.6        - new; pure worst cases / pure
                                            maximin on a 2x2 zero-sum game,
                                            Thm 5.6 scenario
       7. Computing a Maximin Equilibrium - new; same game as 6, mixing;
                                            Battle of the Sexes method
       8. Triple Rock-Paper-Scissors      - variation on book 5.12.4
                                            (rock beating scissors pays 3);
                                            uniqueness as in the poker activity
       9. Tit for Tat                     - based on book 5.12.6

     Confidence checks: Exercises 1(a), 2(a), 4(a), 7(a), 7(b), 8(b), and
     9(c) have a
     <solution> so students can confirm before moving on. Divisional
     solutions are hidden dropdowns (see publication.ptx). -->

<exercises xml:id="ch5-exercises">
  <title>Exercises</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      Many of these exercises use the Fundamental Theorem of Nash
      Equilibria (Theorem 5.2). As a reminder, a mixed strategy profile
      <m>\sigma</m> is a Nash equilibrium exactly when, for every player
      <m>i</m>:
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <p>
          (the <term>equality condition</term>) If <m>s_i</m> and
          <m>s_i'</m> are both active, then
          <m>\pi_i(s_i, \sigma_{-i}) = \pi_i(s_i', \sigma_{-i})</m>.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          (the <term>inequality condition</term>) If <m>s_i</m> is active
          and <m>s_i'</m> is not, then
          <m>\pi_i(s_i, \sigma_{-i}) \geq \pi_i(s_i', \sigma_{-i})</m>.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ol>

    <p>
      A few parts have a <term>Solution</term> dropdown with the final
      answer. Use it to check your answer before moving on, since the
      later parts build on it. But make sure to try the part on your own
      first!
    </p>
    <p>
      Throughout, Player 1 is the row player and Player 2 is the column
      player, and payoffs are listed in the order (Player 1, Player 2).
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <exercise xml:id="hw-compute-msne">
    <title>Computing a Mixed Equilibrium</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        Consider the following game.
      </p>

      <table xml:id="table-hw-compute-msne">
        <title>A <m>2 \times 2</m> game</title>
        <tabular row-headers="yes" halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
          <col halign="left" right="minor" />
          <col right="minor" />
          <col />
          <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
            <cell></cell>
            <cell>Player 2: <m>x</m></cell>
            <cell>Player 2: <m>y</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell>Player 1: <m>a</m></cell>
            <cell>(2, 4)</cell>
            <cell>(0, 0)</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Player 1: <m>b</m></cell>
            <cell>(1, 6)</cell>
            <cell>(3, 7)</cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </table>
    </introduction>

    <task xml:id="hw-compute-msne-find">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Use the Fundamental Theorem of Nash Equilibria to find a mixed
          strategy Nash equilibrium in which both players use both of
          their strategies. Write Player 1's mixed strategy as
          <m>\sigma_1 = pa + (1-p)b</m> and Player 2's as
          <m>\sigma_2 = qx + (1-q)y</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <m>\left(\frac{1}{5}a + \frac{4}{5}b,\ \frac{3}{4}x + \frac{1}{4}y\right)</m>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="hw-compute-msne-payoff">
      <statement>
        <p>
          What is each player's expected payoff at the mixed strategy Nash
          equilibrium you found in part (a)?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <hint>
        <p>
          By Theorem 5.3, a player's equilibrium payoff equals what
          <em>any one</em> of their active strategies earns against the
          other player's mixed strategy. So you can compute, for example,
          <m>\pi_1(a, \sigma_2)</m> instead of adding up all four cells.
        </p>
      </hint>
    </task>

    <conclusion>
      <p>
        <alert>Remark.</alert> This game also has two pure strategy Nash
        equilibria: <m>(a, x)</m>, with payoffs <m>(2, 4)</m>, and
        <m>(b, y)</m>, with payoffs <m>(3, 7)</m>. (You can check these
        with best response.) Comparing these with your answer to part (b),
        we see that both players prefer <m>(b, y)</m> to the mixed
        strategy equilibrium. So a game can have pure and mixed strategy Nash
        equilibria at the same time, and the mixed one isn't necessarily
        better for anyone.
      </p>
    </conclusion>
  </exercise>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <exercise xml:id="hw-symmetric-3x3">
    <title>A Symmetric <m>3 \times 3</m> Game</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        Consider the following two-player game.
      </p>

      <table xml:id="table-hw-symmetric-3x3">
        <title>A symmetric <m>3 \times 3</m> game</title>
        <tabular row-headers="yes" halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
          <col halign="left" right="minor" />
          <col right="minor" />
          <col right="minor" />
          <col />
          <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
            <cell></cell>
            <cell>Player 2: <m>a</m></cell>
            <cell>Player 2: <m>b</m></cell>
            <cell>Player 2: <m>c</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell>Player 1: <m>a</m></cell>
            <cell>(2, 2)</cell>
            <cell>(6, 3)</cell>
            <cell>(5, 4)</cell>
          </row>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell>Player 1: <m>b</m></cell>
            <cell>(3, 6)</cell>
            <cell>(3, 3)</cell>
            <cell>(9, 4)</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Player 1: <m>c</m></cell>
            <cell>(4, 5)</cell>
            <cell>(4, 9)</cell>
            <cell>(7, 7)</cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </table>

      <p>
        This game is <term>symmetric</term>: if the players swap
        strategies, they also swap payoffs. So you only need to do the
        computation for one player, and you get a <term>symmetric Nash
        equilibrium</term> <m>(\sigma, \sigma)</m>, where both players use
        the same mixed strategy <m>\sigma = xa + yb + zc</m>.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <task xml:id="hw-symmetric-3x3-system">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Use the Fundamental Theorem of Nash Equilibria to set up a system
          of linear equations whose solution gives a symmetric Nash
          equilibrium <m>(\sigma, \sigma)</m> in which all three
          strategies are active. Explain where each equation comes from.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <md>
          <mrow>2x + 6y + 5z = 3x + 3y + 9z \amp = 4x + 4y + 7z</mrow>
          <mrow>x + y + z \amp = 1</mrow>
        </md>
      </solution>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="hw-symmetric-3x3-solve">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Solve your system and state the Nash equilibrium
          <m>(\sigma, \sigma)</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <conclusion>
      <p>
        A symmetric game can also have Nash equilibria that are
        <em>not</em> symmetric. You have already seen this: Hawk-Dove
        (<xref ref="hw-hawk-dove-nash" />) is symmetric, and its Nash
        equilibria <m>(h, d)</m> and <m>(d, h)</m> are not symmetric.
      </p>
    </conclusion>
  </exercise>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <exercise xml:id="hw-active-inactive">
    <title>Active and Inactive Strategies</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        Consider the game below, where Player 1 has three strategies and
        Player 2 has two.
      </p>

      <table xml:id="table-hw-active-inactive">
        <title>A <m>3 \times 2</m> game</title>
        <tabular row-headers="yes" halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
          <col halign="left" right="minor" />
          <col right="minor" />
          <col />
          <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
            <cell></cell>
            <cell>Player 2: <m>x</m></cell>
            <cell>Player 2: <m>y</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell>Player 1: <m>a</m></cell>
            <cell>(2, 5)</cell>
            <cell>(6, 4)</cell>
          </row>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell>Player 1: <m>b</m></cell>
            <cell>(5, 5)</cell>
            <cell>(3, 8)</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Player 1: <m>c</m></cell>
            <cell>(3, 7)</cell>
            <cell>(4, 9)</cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </table>

      <p>
        Throughout, write Player 2's mixed strategy as
        <m>\sigma_2 = qx + (1-q)y</m>.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <task xml:id="hw-active-inactive-three">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Use the equality condition of the Fundamental Theorem to show that
          there is no mixed strategy Nash equilibrium in which all three of
          Player 1's strategies are active.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="hw-active-inactive-ab">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find a mixed strategy Nash equilibrium <m>(\sigma_1, \sigma_2)</m>
          in which <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> are active in <m>\sigma_1</m>,
          <m>c</m> is not active in <m>\sigma_1</m>, and <m>x</m> and
          <m>y</m> are both active in <m>\sigma_2</m>. Write
          <m>\sigma_1 = pa + (1-p)b</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <hint>
        <p>
          You must check whether both conditions of Theorem 5.2 can be
          satisfied:
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              The equality condition on <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> determines
              the value of <m>q</m>. The equality condition on <m>x</m> and
              <m>y</m> determines the value of <m>p</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Check the inequality condition for every inactive strategy.
              Notice that <m>c</m> (for Player 1) is the only inactive
              strategy.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </hint>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="hw-active-inactive-ac">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Show that there is no mixed strategy Nash equilibrium in which
          <m>a</m> and <m>c</m> are active in <m>\sigma_1</m> and <m>b</m>
          is inactive.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <hint>
        <p>
          This is similar to part (b), with <m>a</m> and <m>c</m> active
          instead of <m>a</m> and <m>b</m>. Use the equality condition for
          Player 1 to find the only possible <m>\sigma_2</m>. This time, the
          inequality condition should fail for <m>b</m>. (You don't need to
          find <m>\sigma_1</m>.)
        </p>
      </hint>
    </task>

    <conclusion>
      <p>
        <alert>Remark.</alert> In fact, the Nash equilibrium you found in
        part (b) is the <em>only</em> Nash equilibrium of this game. The
        remaining cases (<m>b</m> and <m>c</m> active, pure strategy
        profiles, or one player mixing while the other uses a pure
        strategy) can all be ruled out in the same way.
      </p>
    </conclusion>
  </exercise>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <exercise xml:id="hw-two-stage-pd">
    <title>A Two-Stage Prisoner's Dilemma</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        Consider the following Prisoner's Dilemma, where each player can
        cooperate (<m>c</m>) or defect (<m>d</m>).
      </p>

      <table xml:id="table-hw-two-stage-pd-stage">
        <title>One stage of the game</title>
        <tabular row-headers="yes" halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
          <col halign="left" right="minor" />
          <col right="minor" />
          <col />
          <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
            <cell></cell>
            <cell>Player 2: <m>c</m></cell>
            <cell>Player 2: <m>d</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell>Player 1: <m>c</m></cell>
            <cell>(3, 3)</cell>
            <cell>(-1, 5)</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Player 1: <m>d</m></cell>
            <cell>(5, -1)</cell>
            <cell>(0, 0)</cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </table>

      <p>
        Now the players play this Prisoner's Dilemma <em>twice</em>, and
        each player's total payoff is the sum of their payoffs from the
        two stages. Both players see what happened in the first stage
        before the second stage starts.
      </p>

      <p>
        Because the game has two stages, <m>c</m> and <m>d</m> are now
        <em>moves</em> that a player makes in each stage. A
        <em>strategy</em> for the two-stage game has to say what move to
        make in each stage, and the move in the second stage can depend
        on what happened in the first. We will only consider three
        strategies (there are more). To keep them separate from the
        moves, we write strategies with capital letters:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            <m>C</m>, always cooperate: play <m>c</m> in both stages.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <m>D</m>, always defect: play <m>d</m> in both stages.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <m>T</m>, tit for tat: play <m>c</m> in stage 1. In stage 2,
            copy the move the other player made in stage 1.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        Restricting the players to these three strategies gives the
        following game in normal form, with two entries left blank.
      </p>

      <table xml:id="table-hw-two-stage-pd">
        <title>The two-stage game, to be completed</title>
        <tabular row-headers="yes" halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
          <col halign="left" right="minor" />
          <col right="minor" width="15%" />
          <col right="minor" width="15%" />
          <col width="15%" />
          <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
            <cell></cell>
            <cell>Player 2: <m>C</m></cell>
            <cell>Player 2: <m>D</m></cell>
            <cell>Player 2: <m>T</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell>Player 1: <m>C</m></cell>
            <cell>(6, 6)</cell>
            <cell>(-2, 10)</cell>
            <cell>(6, 6)</cell>
          </row>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell>Player 1: <m>D</m></cell>
            <cell>(10, -2)</cell>
            <cell>(0, 0)</cell>
            <cell></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Player 1: <m>T</m></cell>
            <cell>(6, 6)</cell>
            <cell></cell>
            <cell>(6, 6)</cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </table>
    </introduction>

    <task xml:id="hw-two-stage-pd-fill">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find the payoffs for the strategy profiles <m>(D, T)</m> and
          <m>(T, D)</m>, and fill them in to complete the payoff matrix.
          Briefly explain what happens in each stage.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <m>(D, T)</m> gives <m>(5, -1)</m> and <m>(T, D)</m> gives
          <m>(-1, 5)</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="hw-two-stage-pd-pure">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Use best response to find all pure strategy Nash equilibria.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="hw-two-stage-pd-equality">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Now look for a mixed strategy Nash equilibrium
          <m>(\sigma_1, \sigma_2)</m> in which, for each player, <m>C</m>
          and <m>T</m> are active and <m>D</m> is inactive:
          <me>
            \sigma_1 = pC + (1-p)T, \qquad \sigma_2 = qC + (1-q)T,
            \qquad 0 \lt p \lt 1,\ 0 \lt q \lt 1.
          </me>
          Show that the equality condition of the Fundamental Theorem holds
          for <em>every</em> <m>p</m> and <m>q</m> in <m>(0, 1)</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="hw-two-stage-pd-inequality">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Use the inequality condition to find exactly which values of
          <m>p</m> and <m>q</m> make <m>(\sigma_1, \sigma_2)</m> a mixed
          strategy Nash equilibrium. Then explain in words what your answer
          says: how often can a player use always cooperate (<m>C</m>)
          before the other player would rather switch to always defect
          (<m>D</m>)?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <conclusion>
      <p>
        <alert>Remark.</alert> Like <xref ref="hw-symmetric-3x3" />, this
        is a symmetric game. But here there are infinitely many mixed
        strategy Nash equilibria, and most of them are not symmetric: if
        <m>p</m> and <m>q</m> both satisfy your answer to part (d) but
        <m>p \neq q</m>, the players are using different mixed strategies.
      </p>
    </conclusion>
  </exercise>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <exercise xml:id="hw-simple-poker-mixed">
    <title>A Simple Poker Game, Revisited</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        In <xref ref="hw-simple-poker" /> we found that A Simple Poker
        Game has no Bayesian Nash equilibrium in pure strategies, and we
        promised that it would have one once we allowed mixed strategies.
        Let's find it, this time using the partial derivative method of
        Section 5.3.1 (<xref ref="read-5-3" text="title" />).
      </p>

      <p>
        Recall that Player 1's strategies say what he does with the high
        card and then with the low card. Since <m>FB</m> and <m>FF</m> are
        strictly dominated, we drop them, which leaves the following
        game:
      </p>

      <table xml:id="table-hw-simple-poker-mixed">
        <title>A Simple Poker Game, with <m>FB</m> and <m>FF</m> removed</title>
        <tabular row-headers="yes" halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
          <col halign="left" right="minor" />
          <col right="minor" />
          <col />
          <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
            <cell></cell>
            <cell>Player 2: <m>B</m></cell>
            <cell>Player 2: <m>F</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell>Player 1: <m>BB</m></cell>
            <cell>(0, 0)</cell>
            <cell>(2, -2)</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Player 1: <m>BF</m></cell>
            <cell>(1, -1)</cell>
            <cell>(0, 0)</cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </table>

      <p>
        Write Player 1's mixed strategy as
        <m>\sigma_1 = p\,BB + (1-p)\,BF</m> and Player 2's as
        <m>\sigma_2 = qB + (1-q)F</m>.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <task xml:id="hw-simple-poker-mixed-functions">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Write each player's expected payoff as a function of <m>p</m>
          and <m>q</m>: find <m>\pi_1(p, q)</m> and <m>\pi_2(p, q)</m>, as
          the book does for Tennis in Section 5.3.1.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="hw-simple-poker-mixed-derivatives">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Use the partial derivative method of Section 5.3.1 to find a
          mixed strategy Nash equilibrium with <m>0 \lt p \lt 1</m> and
          <m>0 \lt q \lt 1</m>. Which partial derivatives do you set equal
          to <m>0</m>, and why those ones?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="hw-simple-poker-mixed-payoff">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find Player 1's expected payoff at the Nash equilibrium. Then,
          without doing a second computation, find Player 2's expected
          payoff, and explain why you only needed to compute one of them.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <hint>
        <p>
          In every cell of the table, the two payoffs add to <m>0</m>. So
          for every strategy profile, including mixed ones, Player 2's
          payoff is the negative of Player 1's:
          <m>\pi_2 = -\pi_1</m>.
        </p>
      </hint>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="hw-simple-poker-mixed-interpret">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Interpret the equilibrium:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              What does Player 1 do when he has the high card?
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              When he has the low card, how often does he bid anyway (that
              is, bluff)?
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              How often does Player 2 bid back?
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <conclusion>
      <p>
        <alert>Remark.</alert> Player 2 can never come out ahead in this
        game: none of Player 2's payoffs in the table are positive. The
        best Player 2 can do is keep the losses small, and the equilibrium
        mix does exactly that.
      </p>
    </conclusion>
  </exercise>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <exercise xml:id="hw-maximin-concepts">
    <title>Maximins and Theorem 5.6</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        Consider the following zero-sum game. We'll come back to it in
        <xref ref="hw-maximin-2x2" />.
      </p>

      <table xml:id="table-hw-maximin-2x2">
        <title>A <m>2 \times 2</m> zero-sum game</title>
        <tabular row-headers="yes" halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
          <col halign="left" right="minor" />
          <col right="minor" />
          <col />
          <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
            <cell></cell>
            <cell>Player 2: <m>x</m></cell>
            <cell>Player 2: <m>y</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell>Player 1: <m>a</m></cell>
            <cell>(<m>3</m>, <m>-3</m>)</cell>
            <cell>(<m>-1</m>, <m>1</m>)</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Player 1: <m>b</m></cell>
            <cell>(<m>-2</m>, <m>2</m>)</cell>
            <cell>(<m>1</m>, <m>-1</m>)</cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </table>
    </introduction>

    <task xml:id="hw-maximin-concepts-pure">
      <statement>
        <p>
          First, suppose each player may only use pure strategies.
        </p>
        <ol marker="i.">
          <li>
            <p>
              Find each player's worst case for each of their pure
              strategies: <m>m_1(a)</m> and <m>m_1(b)</m> for Player 1,
              and <m>m_2(x)</m> and <m>m_2(y)</m> for Player 2. For
              example,
              <me>
                m_1(a) = \min_{\sigma_2} \pi_1(a, \sigma_2).
              </me>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Among pure strategies only, which strategy gives each player
              the largest worst case? Call these <m>s_1</m> for Player 1
              and <m>s_2</m> for Player 2. What payoffs
              <m>m_1(s_1)</m> and <m>m_2(s_2)</m> do they guarantee?
              (This is a <q>max of mins</q> like a maximin, but it is
              <em>not</em> the maximin payoff <m>m_1^\dagger</m> from
              Section 5.5, because we're only allowing pure strategies.)
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Is <m>(s_1, s_2)</m> a Nash equilibrium? Do the two
              guaranteed payoffs <m>m_1(s_1)</m> and <m>m_2(s_2)</m> add
              up to <m>0</m>?
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
        <p>
          (Keep your answers in mind. In <xref ref="hw-maximin-2x2" />,
          you'll see what changes when the players can mix.)
        </p>
      </statement>
      <hint>
        <p>
          For i: the minimum is over all of Player 2's strategies,
          including mixed ones. But against a mixed strategy, Player 1's
          payoff is a weighted average of her payoffs against <m>x</m> and
          <m>y</m>, so the worst case is always at a pure strategy.
        </p>

      </hint>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="hw-maximin-concepts-scenario">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Now let's consider a general zero-sum game. Suppose that
          <m>(\sigma_1, \sigma_2)</m> and <m>(\tau_1, \tau_2)</m> are both
          Nash equilibria of a two-player zero-sum game, and that
          <m>\pi_1(\sigma_1, \sigma_2) = 5</m>. Answer each question, and
          say which part of Theorem 5.6 (or the interchangeability
          property, <xref ref="check-interchangeability" />) tells you.
        </p>
        <ol marker="i.">
          <li>
            <p>
              What are <m>m_1^\dagger</m> and <m>m_2^\dagger</m>?
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              What is <m>\pi_1(\tau_1, \tau_2)</m>?
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Is <m>(\sigma_1, \tau_2)</m> a Nash equilibrium? What is
              <m>\pi_2(\sigma_1, \tau_2)</m>?
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Must <m>\sigma_1 = \tau_1</m>?
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </statement>
      <hint>
        <p>
          For iv: does the interchangeability property say the halves of
          two Nash equilibria are <em>equal</em>, or only that you can
          <em>swap</em> them?
        </p>
      </hint>
    </task>

    <conclusion>
      <p>
        <alert>Remark.</alert> In part (a), <m>(s_1, s_2)</m> is not a Nash
        equilibrium, even though the game is zero-sum. This doesn't
        contradict Theorem 5.6. In Section 5.5, <m>\sigma_1</m> and
        <m>\sigma_2</m> range over <em>all</em> strategies, including mixed
        ones, so the maximin strategies and maximin payoffs
        <m>m_i^\dagger</m> in Theorem 5.6 are taken over all mixed
        strategies. As part (a)(ii) points out, <m>s_1</m> and <m>s_2</m>
        are only the best <em>pure</em> strategies, so
        <m>(s_1, s_2)</m> is not a maximin equilibrium. In
        <xref ref="hw-maximin-2x2" />, you'll see that mixing guarantees
        Player 1 more than <m>m_1(s_1)</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        In fact, Theorem 5.6 couldn't hold for pure strategies alone. A pair
        of best pure strategies like <m>(s_1, s_2)</m> always exists, but
        this game has no pure strategy Nash equilibrium. Mixed strategies
        are what make the theorem work.
      </p>
    </conclusion>
  </exercise>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <exercise xml:id="hw-maximin-2x2">
    <title>Computing a Maximin Equilibrium</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        Consider the game from <xref ref="hw-maximin-concepts" />
        (<xref ref="table-hw-maximin-2x2" />). This time the players can use
        mixed strategies. Write Player 1's mixed strategy as
        <m>\sigma_1 = pa + (1-p)b</m> and Player 2's as
        <m>\sigma_2 = qx + (1-q)y</m>. Like the book, we write a strategy
        profile as <m>(p, q)</m>.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <task xml:id="hw-maximin-2x2-worst-case">
      <statement>
        <p>
          We want Player 1's worst case
          <m>m_1(p) = \min_{0 \leq q \leq 1} \pi_1(p, q)</m> for each
          <m>p</m>. Follow the Battle of the Sexes example in the notes
          (starting at <xref ref="table-bos-pq" />).
        </p>
        <ol marker="i.">
          <li>
            <p>
              Show that Player 1's expected payoff is
              <me>
                \pi_1(p, q) = 7pq - 2p - 3q + 1.
              </me>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Hold <m>p</m> fixed and differentiate with respect to
              <m>q</m>. Fill in the blanks:
              <me>
                \frac{\partial \pi_1}{\partial q} = \underline{\qquad\qquad},
                \quad\text{which is }
                \begin{cases}
                  \text{negative} \amp \text{if } 0 \leq p \lt \underline{\qquad}, \\
                  0 \amp \text{if } p = \underline{\qquad}, \\
                  \text{positive} \amp \text{if } \underline{\qquad} \lt p \leq 1.
                \end{cases}
              </me>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Use the sign from (ii) to fill in which value of <m>q</m>
              gives Player 1 her worst case:
              <me>
                m_1(p) =
                \begin{cases}
                  \pi_1(p, \underline{\qquad}) \amp \text{if } 0 \leq p \lt \underline{\qquad}, \\
                  \text{any } \pi_1(p, q) \amp \text{if } p = \underline{\qquad}, \\
                  \pi_1(p, \underline{\qquad}) \amp \text{if } \underline{\qquad} \lt p \leq 1.
                \end{cases}
              </me>
              Then evaluate <m>\pi_1(p, 1)</m> and <m>\pi_1(p, 0)</m>
              using the formula from (i) to write <m>m_1(p)</m> as a
              piecewise function of <m>p</m> alone.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </statement>
      <hint>
        <p>
          If <m>\dfrac{\partial \pi_1}{\partial q}</m> is negative,
          <m>\pi_1(p, q)</m> goes down as <m>q</m> goes up, so it's lowest
          at <m>q = 1</m>. If it's positive, it's lowest at
          <m>q = 0</m>.
        </p>
      </hint>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <m>\dfrac{\partial \pi_1}{\partial q} = 7p - 3</m>, which changes
          sign at <m>p = \frac{3}{7}</m>, and
          <me>
            m_1(p) =
            \begin{cases}
              5p - 2 \amp \text{if } 0 \leq p \lt \frac{3}{7}, \\
              \frac{1}{7} \amp \text{if } p = \frac{3}{7}, \\
              1 - 2p \amp \text{if } \frac{3}{7} \lt p \leq 1.
            \end{cases}
          </me>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="hw-maximin-2x2-player1">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Sketch the graph of <m>m_1(p)</m>. Use it to find Player 1's
          maximin strategy <m>p^\dagger</m> (write it as a mixed strategy
          <m>\sigma_1^\dagger</m> too) and her maximin payoff
          <m>m_1^\dagger</m>. Compare <m>m_1^\dagger</m> with the payoff
          Player 1 could guarantee with pure strategies only, from
          <xref ref="hw-maximin-concepts-pure" />(ii).
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <m>p^\dagger = \frac{3}{7}</m> and
          <m>m_1^\dagger = \frac{1}{7}</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="hw-maximin-2x2-player2">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Now find Player 2's maximin strategy and maximin payoff.
        </p>
        <ol marker="i.">
          <li>
            <p>
              Explain, without doing any computation, why
              <me>
                \pi_2(p, q) = -(7pq - 2p - 3q + 1).
              </me>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Go through the process of part (a) for Player 2 to find her
              worst case
              <m>m_2(q) = \min_{0 \leq p \leq 1} \pi_2(p, q)</m> as a
              piecewise function of <m>q</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Sketch the graph of <m>m_2(q)</m>, and use it to find Player
              2's maximin strategy <m>q^\dagger</m> and her maximin payoff
              <m>m_2^\dagger</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </statement>
      <hint>
        <p>
          For i: the game is zero-sum, and you already know Player 1's
          payoff. For ii: this time hold <m>q</m> fixed and look at the
          sign of <m>\dfrac{\partial \pi_2}{\partial p}</m>.
        </p>
      </hint>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="hw-maximin-2x2-theorem">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Write down the maximin equilibrium
          <m>(p^\dagger, q^\dagger)</m>, and check that
          <m>m_1^\dagger + m_2^\dagger = 0</m>. Then, <em>without</em>
          solving any indifference equations, use Theorem 5.6 to find a
          Nash equilibrium of this game and each player's payoff there (hint: this should be quick!!). Say
          which parts of the theorem you used. Finally, compare with
          <xref ref="hw-maximin-concepts-pure" />: what changed when the
          players were allowed to mix?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <exercise xml:id="hw-triple-rps">
    <title>Triple Rock-Paper-Scissors</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        In ordinary Rock-Paper-Scissors (Problem 5.12.4 in the book),
        paper beats rock, scissors beats paper, and rock beats scissors.
        The winner gets <m>1</m>, the loser gets <m>-1</m>, and a tie
        gives both players <m>0</m>. In <term>Triple
        Rock-Paper-Scissors</term>, a win with rock is worth three times as
        much: when rock beats scissors, the winner gets <m>3</m> and the
        loser gets <m>-3</m>. All other payoffs are the same.
      </p>

      <table xml:id="table-hw-triple-rps">
        <title>Triple Rock-Paper-Scissors</title>
        <tabular row-headers="yes" halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
          <col halign="left" right="minor" />
          <col right="minor" />
          <col right="minor" />
          <col />
          <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
            <cell></cell>
            <cell>Player 2: <m>r</m></cell>
            <cell>Player 2: <m>p</m></cell>
            <cell>Player 2: <m>s</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell>Player 1: <m>r</m></cell>
            <cell>(<m>0</m>, <m>0</m>)</cell>
            <cell>(<m>-1</m>, <m>1</m>)</cell>
            <cell>(<m>3</m>, <m>-3</m>)</cell>
          </row>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell>Player 1: <m>p</m></cell>
            <cell>(<m>1</m>, <m>-1</m>)</cell>
            <cell>(<m>0</m>, <m>0</m>)</cell>
            <cell>(<m>-1</m>, <m>1</m>)</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Player 1: <m>s</m></cell>
            <cell>(<m>-3</m>, <m>3</m>)</cell>
            <cell>(<m>1</m>, <m>-1</m>)</cell>
            <cell>(<m>0</m>, <m>0</m>)</cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </table>

      <p>
        In this exercise, we'll show that Triple Rock-Paper-Scissors has
        exactly one Nash equilibrium, using the same idea as One-Card
        Two-Round Poker (<xref ref="check-poker-uniqueness" />).
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <task xml:id="hw-triple-rps-zero-sum">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Briefly explain why Triple Rock-Paper-Scissors is a zero-sum
          game. (This is what will let us use the interchangeability
          property later.)
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="hw-triple-rps-player1">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Suppose <m>(\sigma_1^*, \sigma_2^*)</m> is a Nash equilibrium in
          which Player 2 uses all three of his strategies. Write
          <m>\sigma_1^* = ar + bp + cs</m>. Player 2's expected payoffs
          against <m>\sigma_1^*</m> are
          <md>
            <mrow>\pi_2(\sigma_1^*, r) \amp = -b + 3c,</mrow>
            <mrow>\pi_2(\sigma_1^*, p) \amp = a - c,</mrow>
            <mrow>\pi_2(\sigma_1^*, s) \amp = -3a + b.</mrow>
          </md>
          (You don't need to derive these, but you can check them against
          the payoff matrix for practice if you want.) Use the Fundamental Theorem,
          with Player 2's indifference equations, to find
          <m>\sigma_1^*</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <m>\sigma_1^* = \frac{1}{5}r + \frac{3}{5}p + \frac{1}{5}s</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="hw-triple-rps-player2">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Notice that your <m>\sigma_1^*</m> uses all three of Player 1's
          strategies. Because they are all active, we can just use Player 1's indifference
          equations to solve for <m>\sigma_2^* </m>. Write <m>\sigma_2^* = xr + yp + zs</m>. Player 1's
          expected payoffs against <m>\sigma_2^*</m> are:
          <md>
            <mrow>\pi_1(r, \sigma_2^*) \amp = -y + 3z,</mrow>
            <mrow>\pi_1(p, \sigma_2^*) \amp = x - z,</mrow>
            <mrow>\pi_1(s, \sigma_2^*) \amp = -3x + y.</mrow>
          </md>
          Use Player 1's indifference equations to find
          <m>\sigma_2^*</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="hw-triple-rps-unique">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Parts (b) and (c) show that if Player 2 uses all three
          strategies, then so does Player 1, and the only Nash equilibrium
          of this kind is <m>(\sigma_1^*, \sigma_2^*)</m>. Use the
          interchangeability property
          (<xref ref="check-interchangeability" />) to explain why this
          implies that Triple Rock-Paper-Scissors has <em>no other</em>
          Nash equilibria.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <hint>
        <p>
          This is the same argument as the poker uniqueness activity
          (<xref ref="check-poker-uniqueness" />), with parts (b) and (c)
          playing the role of the <q>fact from 5.4.</q> Follow the same
          steps, first for Player 1 and then with the players switched.
        </p>
      </hint>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="hw-triple-rps-paper">
      <statement>
        <p>
          In ordinary Rock-Paper-Scissors, the only Nash equilibrium has
          both players use <m>\frac{1}{3}r + \frac{1}{3}p + \frac{1}{3}s</m>.
          In Triple Rock-Paper-Scissors, paper is played most often
          (<m>\frac{3}{5}</m> of the time), even though it's rock's win
          that got bigger. Speculate why (just informally, you don't need any mathematical justification).
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <exercise xml:id="hw-tit-for-tat">
    <title>Tit for Tat</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        This is based on Problem 5.12.6 in the book. Al's and Bob's toy
        stores each set their prices for two weeks in a row. Each store has
        three strategies:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            <m>h</m>: Always charge high prices.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <m>l</m>: Always charge low prices.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <m>t</m>: Tit for tat. Charge high prices the first week. The
            next week, do whatever the other store did the week before.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The total payoffs over the two weeks are:
      </p>

      <table xml:id="table-hw-tit-for-tat">
        <title>Al and Bob's toy stores</title>
        <tabular row-headers="yes" halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
          <col halign="left" right="minor" />
          <col right="minor" />
          <col right="minor" />
          <col />
          <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
            <cell></cell>
            <cell>Bob: <m>h</m></cell>
            <cell>Bob: <m>l</m></cell>
            <cell>Bob: <m>t</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell>Al: <m>h</m></cell>
            <cell>(10, 10)</cell>
            <cell>(0, 12)</cell>
            <cell>(10, 10)</cell>
          </row>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell>Al: <m>l</m></cell>
            <cell>(12, 0)</cell>
            <cell>(6, 6)</cell>
            <cell>(9, 3)</cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Al: <m>t</m></cell>
            <cell>(10, 10)</cell>
            <cell>(3, 9)</cell>
            <cell>(10, 10)</cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </table>
    </introduction>

    <task xml:id="hw-tit-for-tat-tt">
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>(t, t)</m> is a Nash equilibrium. Recall that the Fundamental
          Theorem only requires an inactive strategy to do <em>no
          better</em> than the active ones. Sometimes an inactive strategy
         ties the active ones and does exactly as well. Then the player is
          indifferent to this inactive strategy, so they could mix that strategy in without changing their payoff.
          If the other player's strategy is still a best
          response after that, we get new Nash equilibria! This is the
          book's advice in <xref ref="read-5-6" text="title" />: a tie for
          an inactive strategy is a hint that there are more Nash
          equilibria in which that strategy is active too. (In the
          Ultimatum Minigame, this is how the book found the equilibria
          <m>(f,\ qa + (1-q)r)</m>.)
        </p>
        <p>
          Quickly check that against Bob's <m>t</m>, Al's inactive strategy
          <m>h</m> earns exactly as much as <m>t</m>. (By symmetry, the
          same is true for Bob.)
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="hw-tit-for-tat-equality">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Following the advice, we look for Nash equilibria
          <m>(\sigma, \tau)</m> in which each store mixes <m>h</m> and
          <m>t</m>:
          <me>
            \sigma = ph + (1-p)t, \qquad \tau = qh + (1-q)t,
          </me>
          where <m>0 \lt p \lt 1</m> and <m>0 \lt q \lt 1</m>. Check that
          both indifference equations,
          <me>
            \pi_1(h, \tau) = \pi_1(t, \tau)
            \quad\text{and}\quad
            \pi_2(\sigma, h) = \pi_2(\sigma, t),
          </me>
          hold for every <m>p</m> and <m>q</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="hw-tit-for-tat-inequality">
      <statement>
        <p>
          The only inactive strategy for each store is <m>l</m>. Use the
          second condition of the Fundamental Theorem (the inequality
          condition for <m>l</m>) to find the range of <m>q</m>, and then
          the range of <m>p</m>, for which <m>(\sigma, \tau)</m> is a Nash
          equilibrium. Describe all the Nash equilibria of this form.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <m>0 \lt p \leq \frac{1}{3}</m> and
          <m>0 \lt q \leq \frac{1}{3}</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="hw-tit-for-tat-swap">
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>(l, l)</m> is also a Nash equilibrium, and so is
          <m>(\tau, \tau)</m> with <m>\tau = \frac{1}{3}h + \frac{2}{3}t</m>,
          from part (c). If we swap halves, notice that
          <m>(l, \tau)</m> is <em>not</em> a Nash equilibrium: Bob would
          rather switch to <m>l</m>. Explain why this doesn't contradict
          the interchangeability property
          (<xref ref="check-interchangeability" />).
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>


  <interactive xml:id="question-form-5-ex" iframe="question-form.html?section=chapter 5 exercises" width="100%" aspect="2:1"/>
</exercises>
